WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.260
Speaker 1: Well, we are going to have a very&nbsp;
interesting Senate race for the Democratic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:11.160
primary in California in 2024. Last week, we&nbsp;
talked on the bonus show about Congresswoman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:17.520
Katie Porter announcing that she's running for&nbsp;
Dianne Feinstein's Senate seat. Dianne Feinstein&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:23.700
has been in the Senate about 30 years. She has&nbsp;
not yet actually said she is not running in 2024,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:30.540
but Katie Porter has decided she is going to&nbsp;
run. The latest news is that Adam Schiff is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:36.300
joining that very same race. NBC News reporting.&nbsp;
Adam Schiff running for Senate in California,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:44.160
joining a crowded 2024 primary. So two&nbsp;
members of Congress now at minimum are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:49.140
going to be fighting against each other.&nbsp;
Actually, three because of Barbara Lee.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:55.320
Adam Schiff announced Thursday he's running&nbsp;
for Senate, joining what is expected to be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.320 --> 00:01:00.300
crowded race. Feinstein has not announced her&nbsp;
plans, but already three prominent California&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:06.360
Democrats have set their sights on replacing her.&nbsp;
Schiff and Congress representatives Katie Porter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:12.540
and Barbara Lee. Schiff says Democrats need&nbsp;
a fighter in the Senate who has been at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:17.940
center of the struggle for our democracy and our&nbsp;
economy. He looks forward to campaigning hard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:22.920
meeting Californians where they are and listening&nbsp;
to what they want from their next senator. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:27.480
know, I got a few emails from many of you&nbsp;
fine folks saying, David, isn't this isn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:33.420
this dangerous? Isn't this potentially going to&nbsp;
lose a Senate seat for Democrats in California?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:38.640
I don't think that there's any risk of that.&nbsp;
I really don't. I think a hard fought primary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:49.140
is fine and should be welcomed in most cases.&nbsp;
And California is very blue and in almost.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:58.020
For sure terms. We can say that the winner of that&nbsp;
Democratic primary will be the next senator from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:03.120
California. For me, the more interesting question&nbsp;
is, are these folks who are going to be running&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:08.040
against Dianne Feinstein? And I think there's two&nbsp;
dynamics at play that are going on now. Many this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:12.180
is not the type of thing we make fun of. There&nbsp;
is a reality that there have been real concerns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:19.380
cognitively about Dianne Feinstein for a couple of&nbsp;
years, reports that she will meet people regularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:24.060
in her course of being a senator and not remember&nbsp;
that she's met them before or multiple times.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:30.960
We've even seen very kind of tough to watch&nbsp;
public situations where Feinstein will read a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:37.200
question to a witness during testimony, during&nbsp;
a hearing, and then immediately read the exact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:41.300
same question a second time with no recognition&nbsp;
that she's already asked that question. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:47.700
are real questions she's getting to to you know,&nbsp;
she's getting up there in age and we want to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:52.080
sensitive to all these things. Part of this&nbsp;
may be an effort to push her out by saying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:56.940
look, there's so many people running. There's&nbsp;
not even really a guarantee you would win. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.940 --> 00:03:03.540
is a good time to maybe maybe move on. The other&nbsp;
speculation about why we are seeing so much early&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:09.000
announcement for this seat is that some of these&nbsp;
folks, Katie Porter, Barbara Lee, Adam Schiff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:16.800
they may hope that by announcing early they will&nbsp;
keep others from entering the race, by showing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:21.120
look, I'm a strong candidate, I'm well known, I&nbsp;
have a good shot at this thing. Don't even bother&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:26.400
getting involved. And I don't know whether anyone&nbsp;
has been dissuaded so far from entering that race&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:29.760
because of the early announcements. But it is&nbsp;
going to be a very interesting race. It's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:35.280
going to be a race that will be material in terms&nbsp;
of the balance. If Dianne Feinstein doesn't run,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:39.720
she's going to be replaced by by one of&nbsp;
these Democrats because of California's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:44.220
voting demographics. And so in that sense,&nbsp;
it won't be interesting to the balance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:48.720
but it will be an interesting race to see the&nbsp;
direction that Californians are are leaning.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:56.820
I don't have a strong opinion about these three&nbsp;
right now, nor do I vote in California. So I am&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.820 --> 00:04:01.740
not inclined to come out right now and say,&nbsp;
here is the one of these three that should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:05.220
definitely be supported. I'm actually interested&nbsp;
interested in seeing how the campaign rolls on

